@*
    Commercial airliner silhouette, seen from above, nose pointing along +x.

    Drawn as one path so it can be dropped into any SVG and rotated by offset-rotate along a flight
    path. The proportions are what make it read as an airliner rather than as an arrow or a rocket:
    a tapered nose, a constant-width fuselage, swept wings set forward of centre, and a separate
    swept tailplane at the back. The earlier abstract dart had none of those and looked like a
    missile, which is the specific thing this replaces.

    Nose-at-+x matters: CSS offset-rotate aligns the element's positive x axis with the path tangent,
    so an aircraft authored pointing any other way flies sideways.
*@

<g class="@Class" transform="scale(@Scale.ToString(System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture))">
    <path fill="@Fill"
          d="M18 0
             C16 -1.2 13 -1.9 9 -2.1
             L5 -2.1 L-5 -12.5 L-8.5 -12.5 L-2.5 -2.1
             L-10 -2.1 L-14.5 -7.5 L-17 -7.5 L-13.5 -2
             L-16 -1.6 L-16 1.6 L-13.5 2
             L-17 7.5 L-14.5 7.5 L-10 2.1
             L-2.5 2.1 L-8.5 12.5 L-5 12.5 L5 2.1
             L9 2.1 C13 1.9 16 1.2 18 0 Z" />
</g>

@code {
    /// <summary>Fill for the silhouette. Defaults to the surrounding colour.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string Fill { get; set; } = "currentColor";

    /// <summary>Uniform scale. The path is authored at roughly 34 units nose to tail.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public double Scale { get; set; } = 1;

    /// <summary>Class applied to the group, used to attach the flight animation.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Class { get; set; }
}
